<description>
Rotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.
Input structure
- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.
- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.
Rule
- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.
- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.
- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.
Key insight
- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars on the background; blue bars start chains and red bars may follow",
  "transform": "for each blue-started chain, rotate each reached vertical bar clockwise onto the shared baseline; the next red bar is reached when its column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 3-9 rows x 7-30 cols, bg=0
  bars:
    description: bottom-aligned 1-cell-wide vertical bars, all sharing same baseline (last row)
    colors: blue(1) = marker, red(2) = body; only these two colors
    count: 2-9 total, 1-3 blue + 1-6 red
    height: 2-4 cells each; grid rows >= max bar height
    no_overlap: each bar occupies a unique column
  chains:
    rule: starting at each blue bar, greedily chain the next bar if it is red AND its column == current bar's column + current bar's height
    count: 1-3 chains (one per blue bar)
    length: 1-5 bars per chain (including the blue starter)
    fit: last bar's col + height <= grid width (rotated chain must fit)
  unchained_red:
    count: 0-2 red bars not reached by any chain
    reason: gap from preceding bar != that bar's height, or preceding bar is unchained red
    behavior: remain unchanged in output [observed]
  transform:
    clear: erase all vertical cells of each chained bar
    draw: on baseline row, fill [col, col+height) with bar's color for each chained bar
    unchained bars and empty space are unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid rows (3-9) and number of blue bars (1-3).
    2. For each blue bar, build a chain (1-5 bars). Assign heights (2-4) per bar. The chain occupies a contiguous horizontal segment on the baseline: sum of heights = segment width.
    3. Optionally create 0-2 unchained red bars with heights 2-4.
    4. Lay out all chains and unchained bars left-to-right with gaps between groups. Unchained red bars must NOT satisfy the chaining rule from any predecessor (ensure gap != predecessor's height, or predecessor is another unchained red).
    5. Set grid width = rightmost bar's col + height + random margin (0-2).
    6. Build input: place each bar vertically (bottom-aligned) at its assigned column with its assigned height.
    7. Verify grid width <= 30 and all constraints hold.
```
